    "['neighbor [parameter1] remote-as [parameter2]', [{'para_map': [-1, 0], 'trans_command': 'type [parameter2]', 'parent_command': ['set protocols bgp group [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set protocols bgp group [parameter1]'}, {'para_map': [0, 0], 'trans_command': 'neighbor [parameter2] peer-as [parameter3]', 'parent_command': ['set protocols bgp group [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set protocols bgp group [parameter1]'}, {'para_map': [1, 1], 'trans_command': 'neighbor [parameter2] peer-as [parameter3]', 'parent_command': ['set protocols bgp group [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set protocols bgp group [parameter1]'}, {'para_map': [-1, -1], 'trans_command': 'export [parameter2]', 'parent_command': ['set protocols bgp group [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set protocols bgp group [parameter1]'}]]": 1,
    "['address-family [parameter1] [parameter2]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'family inet unicast', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'set protocols bgp group [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the equivalent Juniper configuration command for `address-family ipv4 unicast`:\\n\\n##\\nfamily inet unicast\\n##\\n\\nIn Juniper, the `family inet` specifies IPv4 addressing, and `unicast` is the default address family type for IPv4. This would typically appear within a BGP neighbor configuration or policy context.\", 'trans_template': 'family inet unicast'}]]": 1,
    "['no synchronization', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'no synchronization', 'parent_command': ['family inet unicast'], 'root': 'set protocols bgp group [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"##\\nno synchronization\\n##\\n\\nFor Juniper, the equivalent command would be to disable BGP synchronization, which is not explicitly needed since Juniper's BGP implementation does not perform synchronization by default (unlike Cisco's legacy behavior). However, if you want to explicitly ensure it's disabled in Juniper, you could use:\\n\\n##\\nset protocols bgp group <group-name> no-synchronize\\n##\\n\\nBut since the original Cisco command is just `no synchronization`, and Juniper doesn't require this by default, a direct translation isn't strictly necessary. The closest explicit equivalent is the `no-synchronize` statement under the BGP group.\", 'trans_template': 'no synchronization'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set protocols bgp group <group-name> no-synchronize', 'parent_command': ['family inet unicast'], 'root': 'set protocols bgp group [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"##\\nno synchronization\\n##\\n\\nFor Juniper, the equivalent command would be to disable BGP synchronization, which is not explicitly needed since Juniper's BGP implementation does not perform synchronization by default (unlike Cisco's legacy behavior). However, if you want to explicitly ensure it's disabled in Juniper, you could use:\\n\\n##\\nset protocols bgp group <group-name> no-synchronize\\n##\\n\\nBut since the original Cisco command is just `no synchronization`, and Juniper doesn't require this by default, a direct translation isn't strictly necessary. The closest explicit equivalent is the `no-synchronize` statement under the BGP group.\", 'trans_template': 'set protocols bgp group <group-name> no-synchronize'}]]": 1,
    "['redistribute connected', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set policy-options policy-statement REDISTRIBUTE-CONNECTED term 1 from protocol direct', 'parent_command': ['family inet unicast'], 'root': 'set protocols bgp group [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\nset policy-options policy-statement REDISTRIBUTE-CONNECTED term 1 from protocol direct\\nset policy-options policy-statement REDISTRIBUTE-CONNECTED then accept\\nset protocols bgp group EBGP export REDISTRIBUTE-CONNECTED\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\n1. In Juniper, route redistribution is handled through policy statements\\n2. \"direct\" is the Juniper equivalent of Cisco\\'s \"connected\" routes\\n3. The policy is created first (REDISTRIBUTE-CONNECTED), then applied to BGP as an export policy\\n4. This achieves the same result as Cisco\\'s \"redistribute connected\" by exporting directly connected routes into BGP', 'trans_template': 'set policy-options policy-statement REDISTRIBUTE-CONNECTED term 1 from protocol direct'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set policy-options policy-statement REDISTRIBUTE-CONNECTED then accept', 'parent_command': ['family inet unicast'], 'root': 'set protocols bgp group [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\nset policy-options policy-statement REDISTRIBUTE-CONNECTED term 1 from protocol direct\\nset policy-options policy-statement REDISTRIBUTE-CONNECTED then accept\\nset protocols bgp group EBGP export REDISTRIBUTE-CONNECTED\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\n1. In Juniper, route redistribution is handled through policy statements\\n2. \"direct\" is the Juniper equivalent of Cisco\\'s \"connected\" routes\\n3. The policy is created first (REDISTRIBUTE-CONNECTED), then applied to BGP as an export policy\\n4. This achieves the same result as Cisco\\'s \"redistribute connected\" by exporting directly connected routes into BGP', 'trans_template': 'set policy-options policy-statement REDISTRIBUTE-CONNECTED then accept'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set protocols bgp group EBGP export REDISTRIBUTE-CONNECTED', 'parent_command': ['family inet unicast'], 'root': 'set protocols bgp group [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\nset policy-options policy-statement REDISTRIBUTE-CONNECTED term 1 from protocol direct\\nset policy-options policy-statement REDISTRIBUTE-CONNECTED then accept\\nset protocols bgp group EBGP export REDISTRIBUTE-CONNECTED\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\n1. In Juniper, route redistribution is handled through policy statements\\n2. \"direct\" is the Juniper equivalent of Cisco\\'s \"connected\" routes\\n3. The policy is created first (REDISTRIBUTE-CONNECTED), then applied to BGP as an export policy\\n4. This achieves the same result as Cisco\\'s \"redistribute connected\" by exporting directly connected routes into BGP', 'trans_template': 'set protocols bgp group EBGP export REDISTRIBUTE-CONNECTED'}]]": 1,
